Basics of Git:

✅ What is a Git Repository?
A Git repository is a storage space where your code, history, branches, tags, and versioned files live.
•Local Repository – On your machine (via git init or git clone)
•Remote Repository – On GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket (e.g., https://lnkd.in/gdVY8H8k)

✅ What is a Git Branch?
A branch is a pointer to a snapshot of your changes. It lets you work on features, fixes, or experiments without affecting the main codebase.
•Think of main or master as the default stable branch.
•Other branches (e.g., feature/login, bugfix/api-crash) are created for isolated work.

✅ Common Branch Types:
•main or master- Stable production code   
•develop- Integration of features before going to production   
•feature/* -New features being developed   
•bugfix/* -Fixing minor issues before release   
•hotfix/*-Urgent fixes directly to production   
•release/*-Pre-release stabilization (QA, tests, documentation)  

✅ Basic Git Commands:
•git clone – Copies a remote repository to your local machine.
•git pull – Fetches changes from the remote repo and merges them into your current branch.
•git push – Uploads your local commits to the remote repository.
•git status – Shows the state of your working directory and staged files.
•git add – Stages changes (files) to be included in the next commit.
•git commit – Saves your staged changes to the local repository with a message.
•git checkout – Switches between branches or restores files in the working directory.
•git merge – Combines changes from one branch into another.
•git log – Displays a history of commits in the repository.
•git diff – Shows the difference between two commits, branches, or working states..
